Scene Validation & Export Tool for Blender to Unreal Engine

When preparing assets inside Blender to then integrate to Unreal Engine we can often run
into errors during the process, whether its making correct naming conventions, applying
transformations and scale and many more little things, we end up wasting a lot of time
getting everything prepared to then bring it to Unreal.

This tool aims to solve all these issues for environment / tech artists by optimizing this
process. Through a UI that takes you through scene validation to check all the possible
little errors made along the way, to automatic cleanup where we have the option to
quickly resolve these issues (ex. correcting naming conventions "object_01 ->
SM_Object_01"), to exporting to unreal engine and automatically setting up the appropriate
folder structures.

Our tool will work as a Production Bridge between our Primary Software : Blender and our
Secondary Software : Unreal Engine.

The language for this tool will be Python using Blender's bpy API:
• INPUT: Selected objects / Full scene in Blender
• OUTPUT: Exported validated assets as .fbx organized in a structured folder system
ready to be used in Unreal Engine

Our Core Features will consist of:
• Scene Validation : Detecting objects with un-applied transforms and scaling,
identifying incorrect naming conventions, detecting duplicates / unused materials.
Reset or keep current to default (scale:1/ rotation:0), location of pivot point
can be controlled to keep at world center 0 or the current pivot.
• Clean up System : Applying transforms and scaling, renaming objects based on
preferred naming conventions, merging/removing duplicate materials.
• Export System : Export selected objects or scene as an .fbx, automatically
organizing the exported files into the appropriate folders, making sure the export
settings are correct and compatible for Unreal Engine. Auto importer in Unreal,
that also adds proper naming convention (SM_ prefix for example).

The Modular Add-ons we could implement in the process of creating this tool or in the
future could be:
• Poly-count checker that warns when encountering high density meshes.
• Automatic removal of unused materials.
• Presets for different export settings to not only export for Unreal Engine.
• Auto-generate materials for Unreal Engine.